>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>A career in home science in India covers six real tracks: dietetics and clinical nutrition, food science and product development, textile and apparel design, child development and early education, extension work inside government nutrition programs, and hospitality and catering management.</strong>
    It is not the cooking-and-stitching subject it still gets mistaken for.
    The best track for you depends on whether you want a structured salary ladder, the highest long-term ceiling, or a real path toward owning your own practice or brand — not on which track sounds the most respectable at a family dinner.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Home science splits into distinct, real career tracks — dietetics, food technology, textile design, child development, extension education, and hospitality management — each with different pay and training paths.</li>
      <li>Food science and food technology carry the strongest structured salary ladder; dietetics carries the highest realistic ceiling through private practice, but the slowest start.</li>
      <li>Textile design, a nutrition practice, and a food business are all genuine ownership routes in this field — the kind of leverage that builds a high-value skill portfolio and moves you toward earlier financial freedom faster than a fixed salary alone.</li>
      <li>Government nutrition roles like Anganwadi Supervisor and CDPO offer real stability, but the pay is structurally capped and the CDPO route specifically involves a genuinely competitive, multi-stage selection process.</li>
      <li><strong>Run any track through the 4-Checkpoint Protocol and the 3 Gates before committing years and money to it.</strong></li>
    </ul>
