  <h2 id="stereotype">The stereotype a career in home science still has to fight</h2>

  <p>
    Say "home science" out loud in most Indian households and the image that shows up is cooking classes and stitching.
  </p>

  <p>
    That image is decades out of date, and it is doing real damage.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The usual dismissive line</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>It's just cooking and sewing.</li>
      <li>It's a subject for girls who don't want a "real" career.</li>
      <li>There's no serious money in it.</li>
      <li>Anything with "science" attached is worth more than home science.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of this reflects what the degree actually leads to today, or what the real pay in each track looks like.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Modern home science — some universities now call it Family and Community Science — pulls in biology, chemistry, psychology, sociology, economics, design, and communication.
  </p>

  <p>
    It leads to clinical dietitians, food safety professionals, textile technologists, fashion designers, early-years educators, family counsellors, NGO and policy roles, and business owners running food, nutrition, or design ventures.
  </p>

  <p>
    A large part of why the stereotype survives is enrolment pattern, not job reality: home science undergraduate programs in India are overwhelmingly female-enrolled, and that pattern gets read backward as proof the field is "only for women" or somehow less serious.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is neither. It is a multidisciplinary degree that opens into genuinely different careers depending on which track you specialise into — and that is the real decision this article is here to help you make.
  </p>

