  <h2 id="dietetics">Dietetics and clinical nutrition: the slow-start, highest-ceiling track</h2>

  <p>
    Dietetics is the track most people picture when they hear "nutrition career," and it is also the one most misunderstood on pay.
  </p>

  <p>
    A hospital-employed clinical dietitian's salary in India is genuinely modest to start — job-portal data puts the average close to ₹20,000-25,000 a month, with some city variation and higher pay in corporate wellness platforms.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    {dietComparisonCards.map((card) => (
      <div class:list={['compare-card', `compare-card--${card.tone}`]}>
        <div class="compare-card__label">{card.title}</div>
        <ul>
          {card.items.map((item) => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take: the multi-stage competition here is real</p>
    <p>
      Clinical dietetics has two separate hard stages, not one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first is getting into a recognised postgraduate diploma or master's program in dietetics — seats at reputable government institutes are few and entrance-exam gated. The second, separate stage comes after: building a private-practice client base takes years of visible results and word of mouth, and most practitioners run it alongside salaried work rather than as a sole income from day one. Clearing the first stage does not automatically clear the second.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    None of this means dietetics is a weak choice. It means the honest plan has two phases, and you should budget time and money for both instead of assuming the postgraduate seat alone is the finish line.
  </p>

  <h2 id="food-science">Food science, food technology and product development</h2>

  <p>
    This is the track with the most conventional, predictable pay ladder in the entire home science family.
  </p>

  <p>
    Food technologists typically start around ₹4.5 LPA, with average pay in the ₹5.5-7.3 LPA range and experienced professionals in R&D, quality, or regulatory roles reaching ₹18-30 LPA. Product Development roles specifically average close to ₹7.8 LPA. Real employers include Britannia, Dabur, Cadbury, and the Food Corporation of India, along with the wider FMCG and food-processing sector.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    If you already know you want the food-technology route specifically rather than the broader home science degree, read <a href="/blog/career-options/is-food-technology-a-good-career-in-india/">is food technology a good career in India</a> for the dedicated deep dive.
  </p>

  <p>
    The reason this track sits at the top of the growth-headroom list is not just the salary number. It is that the same skill set — understanding formulation, shelf life, ingredients, and food safety compliance — is exactly what a founder needs to launch a packaged food or specialty food brand, which is one of the more common small-business routes coming out of this field in India.
  </p>

  <h2 id="textile">Textile and apparel design: craft plus a real label route</h2>

  <p>
    Home science's textile and clothing specialisation covers fabric construction, dyeing, printing, and performance testing — the technical side of textiles, not just fashion sketching.
  </p>

  <p>
    Salaried textile and apparel design pay moves from roughly ₹2.5-4.6 LPA at entry (graduates from stronger institutes report closer to ₹5 LPA) to ₹5-9 LPA mid-career and ₹10-18 LPA past six years of experience. Freelance work, once a portfolio and client base exist, spans a much wider ₹2-30 LPA range depending on skill, niche, and reputation.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    For the fashion-design side of this specifically, see <a href="/blog/career-options/is-fashion-designing-a-good-career-in-india/">is fashion designing a good career in India</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    That freelance ceiling, plus the option to build a personal label sold through an online store rather than only through wholesale buyers, is what earns this track its position ahead of hospitality and government routes on the growth-headroom list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="child-dev">Child development and early childhood education</h2>

  <p>
    India's urban preschool, daycare, and early-years education sector has grown meaningfully over the last decade, and home science graduates with a child development specialisation are a natural fit for it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest constraint is salary. Payscale data for early childhood educators in India shows an overall average close to ₹3 LPA, with entry-level pay slightly below that and a meaningful jump only appearing at senior or leadership level. A classroom teaching role alone, without a leadership or ownership step, has a genuinely slow salary curve.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The real scalable move in this track is ownership, not seniority inside someone else's centre.
    </p>
    <p>
      Founding or co-owning a preschool or daycare centre — with proper hiring, curriculum, and repeat enrolment — is where the income ceiling actually changes, because you are then earning from a business, not a single classroom salary.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="human-dev">Human development, family studies and counselling-adjacent work</h2>

  <p>
    This track suits students genuinely interested in behaviour, relationships, and family systems more than food, textiles, or product work.
  </p>

  <p>
    Be clear-eyed about one thing: a home science background in human development is not the same as a licence to practise as a psychologist or family therapist. Ethical, credible private counselling work requires an additional recognised psychology or counselling qualification layered on top.
  </p>

  <p>
    Without that added credential, realistic paid work tends to run through NGO project roles, CSR-funded community programs, school counsellor support positions, or research assistance — genuinely useful work, but narrower and slower to monetise than the other tracks on this list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="extension">Extension education and government nutrition programs</h2>

  <p>
    This is the track most closely tied to India's public nutrition and child-welfare system — the Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS) scheme, Anganwadi-linked posts, and food-safety regulation through FSSAI.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Role</th>
          <th>Requirement</th>
          <th>Reality</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {icdsLadderRows.map((item) => (
