  <h2 id="ai">AI and home science careers: what gets automated, what doesn't</h2>

  <p>
    Home science students often assume AI is a technology-sector problem. It already isn't.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Getting easier to automate</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Generic, templated diet or meal plans that are not tailored to a specific medical condition or client history.</li>
        <li>Routine nutrition content — basic recipe write-ups, generic calorie-counting explanations, and standard food-fact posts.</li>
        <li>First-draft product concept notes and repetitive documentation work in food safety and quality systems.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Staying human for now</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Clinical judgment that combines a patient's medical history, lab values, and personal circumstances into one safe, workable plan.</li>
        <li>The trust and relationship-building that make a client renew a nutrition, counselling, or design engagement.</li>
        <li>Sensory and taste judgment in food product development, and aesthetic judgment in textile and apparel design.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The realistic risk is not "AI replaces dietitians." It is that the most basic, templated part of nutrition and content work becomes commoditised, which pushes value toward practitioners who combine real clinical or design judgment with a genuine client relationship — exactly the part AI does not replicate well.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Right now:</strong> use one AI tool to speed up first-draft meal plans, product notes, or content, and check every output yourself before it reaches a client.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Later, as tools mature:</strong> the bigger opportunity is building or using AI-assisted meal-planning and client-management tools to serve more clients per week, and using content or education platforms to reach people beyond your immediate city — both of which strengthen the private-practice and business-ownership routes described earlier in this article, rather than replacing them.
    </p>
  </div>

